# Day 4 - Customer Domain Model & Oracle Persistence

## Enterprise Financial Data Platform

### Objective

The goal of Day 4 was to move the Customer Service from a basic Spring Boot service into a real database-backed microservice.

The Customer Service was integrated with Oracle using Spring Data JPA and Hibernate. A Customer domain entity, repository layer, service layer, DTO layer, validation foundation, mapper, and database-backed REST API were implemented and tested end-to-end.

---

## 1. Architecture Implemented

The Customer Service now follows a standard layered Spring Boot architecture.

```text
Postman / Future Angular UI
          |
          | HTTP Request
          v
CustomerController
          |
          v
CustomerService
          |
          v
CustomerRepository
          |
          v
Spring Data JPA
          |
          v
JPA
          |
          v
Hibernate
          |
          v
Oracle JDBC Driver
          |
          v
Oracle Database - FREEPDB1
          |
          v
CUSTOMERS Table
```

Each layer has a separate responsibility.

- Controller handles HTTP requests and responses.
- Service coordinates business logic.
- Repository handles persistence operations.
- Spring Data JPA provides repository abstractions.
- JPA defines the persistence standard.
- Hibernate implements JPA and performs object-relational mapping.
- JDBC provides database connectivity.
- Oracle stores the actual customer records.

