## 11. JPA Entity Annotations

The Customer class uses JPA annotations.

### @Entity

```java
@Entity
```

Marks the Java class as a persistent JPA entity.

---

### @Table

```java
@Table(name = "CUSTOMERS")
```

Maps the Customer entity to the Oracle `CUSTOMERS` table.

---

### @Id

```java
@Id
```

Identifies the primary key of the entity.

---

### @GeneratedValue

```java
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
```

Indicates that the database generates the primary-key value.

---

### @Column

Example:

```java
@Column(name = "CUSTOMER_NUMBER", nullable = false, unique = true)
```

Maps a Java field to a specific database column and allows persistence constraints to be described.

---

## 12. Lombok Getter and Setter

The Customer entity uses:

```java
@Getter
@Setter
```

These are Lombok annotations.

Lombok generates getter and setter methods during compilation and avoids writing repetitive accessor methods manually.

The Customer entity also keeps a no-argument constructor because JPA requires an accessible no-argument constructor when creating entity instances.

