## DTO, Validation & Mapper Foundation

Day 4 was extended to introduce a proper API contract instead of exposing the JPA entity directly.

### CustomerRequest

Created:

```text
customer/dto/CustomerRequest.java
```

`CustomerRequest` represents incoming customer data and defines Jakarta Bean Validation rules.

Current validation annotations include:

- `@NotBlank` for required first and last names and email.
- `@Size` for name length validation.
- `@Email` for email-format validation.
- `@Pattern` for the expected 10-digit phone-number format.
- `@Past` for date-of-birth validation.

Server-controlled fields such as `customerId`, `customerNumber`, `customerStatus`, `createdAt`, and `updatedAt` are intentionally not accepted in the request DTO.

These validation rules are prepared on Day 4 and will execute when the request DTO is connected to an endpoint using `@Valid`.

### CustomerResponse

Created:

```text
customer/dto/CustomerResponse.java
```

`CustomerResponse` defines the public customer API response and prevents the JPA entity from becoming the external API contract.

### CustomerMapper

Created:

```text
customer/mapper/CustomerMapper.java
```

`CustomerMapper` converts the persistence entity into the response DTO:

```text
Customer Entity
      ↓
CustomerMapper.toResponse()
      ↓
CustomerResponse DTO
```

The mapper is currently a plain Java utility class.

### Updated GET Flow

The database-backed GET API was refactored to use the response DTO:

```text
Oracle CUSTOMERS
       ↓
Customer Entity
       ↓
CustomerRepository
       ↓
CustomerService
       ↓
CustomerMapper
       ↓
CustomerResponse DTO
       ↓
CustomerController
       ↓
ApiResponse<List<CustomerResponse>>
       ↓
JSON
```

This provides separation between the persistence model and REST API contract.

